<sup><b>1</b></sup> But Job answered and said,<br />
<sup><b>2</b></sup> How hast thou helped <i>him that is</i> without power? <i>how</i> savest thou the arm <i>that hath</i> no strength?<br />
<sup><b>3</b></sup> How hast thou counselled <i>him that hath</i> no wisdom? and <i>how</i> hast thou plentifully declared the thing as it is?<br />
<sup><b>4</b></sup> To whom hast thou uttered words? and whose spirit came from thee?<br />
<sup><b>5</b></sup> Dead <i>things</i> are formed from under the waters, and the inhabitants thereof.<br />
<sup><b>6</b></sup> Hell <i>is</i> naked before him, and destruction hath no covering.<br />
<sup><b>7</b></sup> He stretcheth out the north over the empty place, <i>and</i> hangeth the earth upon nothing.<br />
<sup><b>8</b></sup> He bindeth up the waters in his thick clouds; and the cloud is not rent under them.<br />
<sup><b>9</b></sup> He holdeth back the face of his throne, <i>and</i> spreadeth his cloud upon it.<br />
<sup><b>10</b></sup> He hath compassed the waters with bounds, until the day and night come to an end.<br />
<sup><b>11</b></sup> The pillars of heaven tremble and are astonished at his reproof.<br />
<sup><b>12</b></sup> He divideth the sea with his power, and by his understanding he smiteth through the proud.<br />
<sup><b>13</b></sup> By his spirit he hath garnished the heavens; his hand hath formed the crooked serpent.<br />
<sup><b>14</b></sup> Lo, these <i>are</i> parts of his ways: but how little a portion is heard of him? but the thunder of his power who can understand? <br />
